WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.537
Secure Java upload handling starts with
defining allowed use cases before accepting

2
00:00:04.617 --> 00:00:04.954
files.

3
00:00:05.034 --> 00:00:08.734
Identify who can upload, what file types
are allowed, why the files are needed,

4
00:00:08.814 --> 00:00:12.178
how large they can be, where they are
stored, how long they are retained,

5
00:00:12.258 --> 00:00:14.894
who can retrieve them, and what processing
is required.

6
00:00:14.974 --> 00:00:19.179
Require appropriate authentication and
authorization for both uploads and downloads.

7
00:00:19.259 --> 00:00:22.904
Treat file upload as a formal security
boundary, not a convenience endpoint.

8
00:00:22.984 --> 00:00:26.796
Configure multipart handling with explicit
limits for file size, request size,

9
00:00:26.876 --> 00:00:30.297
part count, memory threshold, temporary
location, timeouts, and rate.

10
00:00:30.377 --> 00:00:34.021
Validate file type with allowlists and
multiple signals where appropriate.

11
00:00:34.101 --> 00:00:37.858
Generate server-side storage names and
treat original filenames as untrusted

12
00:00:37.938 --> 00:00:38.443
metadata.

13
00:00:38.523 --> 00:00:41.663
Store files outside executable, classpath,
deployment, source,

14
00:00:41.743 --> 00:00:45.611
and public web-root locations unless
public access is the explicit design and the

15
00:00:45.691 --> 00:00:46.701
controls support it.

16
00:00:46.781 --> 00:00:50.369
Use quarantine, scanning, and isolated
processing for higher-risk files.

17
00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:53.309
Process untrusted content with least
privilege, timeouts,

18
00:00:53.389 --> 00:00:57.314
resource limits, constrained temporary
directories, and safe failure behavior.

19
00:00:57.394 --> 00:01:01.543
Apply object-level authorization to
downloads, previews, signed URLs, and metadata.

20
00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:05.268
Protect upload systems with quotas, rate
limits, cleanup jobs, monitoring,

21
00:01:05.348 --> 00:01:07.759
safe error responses, and operational
ownership.

22
00:01:07.839 --> 00:01:11.035
A practical first-week plan is clear:
inventory upload endpoints,

23
00:01:11.115 --> 00:01:13.918
confirm allowed types and sizes, review
multipart limits,

24
00:01:13.998 --> 00:01:16.409
check storage paths, verify generated
filenames,

25
00:01:16.489 --> 00:01:19.909
add download authorization checks, review
logging for sensitive data,

26
00:01:19.989 --> 00:01:21.335
test scanner failure paths,

27
00:01:21.415 --> 00:01:24.443
and document the response path for
suspicious or exposed files.

28
00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:28.280
Then build upload review into pull
requests, tests, CI/CD, deployment review,

29
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.780
and incident response so the controls stay
current as the feature grows.
